  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nA robust plume of monsoonal moisture continues to surge into \neastern Idaho this afternoon, setting the stage for an active 24\nto 36 hours across the region. Isolated to scattered showers \nand thunderstorms will continue to develop through the remainder\nof the afternoon and persist into the early overnight hours. \nWhile a brief lull in convective coverage is possible early \nWednesday morning, a secondary, potent wave of deep atmospheric \nmoisture will stream into the area shortly thereafter. \n\nPrecipitable Water (PWAT) values on Wednesday will climb well \nabove climatological norms, approaching the 99th percentile for \nlate July. This atmospheric profile will yield exceptionally \nefficient, heavy-rainfall-producing showers and thunderstorms. \nGiven the slow movement of these storms and high rain rates, \nlocalized flash flooding is a major concern. Consequently, a \nFlood Watch has been issued for the Eastern and Southeastern \nHighlands, as well as the Wapiti Burn Scar across the central \nmountains where debris flows will be a heightened risk. While \nthe watch area currently encompasses the highest confidence for \nheavy rain, areas outside the watch across eastern Idaho remain \nvulnerable to localized flash flooding if slow-moving cores \ntrack directly overhead. Widespread cloud cover and \nprecipitation will limit diurnal heating, capping Wednesday \nhighs in the 80s for lower elevations and the mid-to-upper 70s \nacross higher terrain. Gusty outflow winds and small hail will \nalso accompany stronger cells. \n\nWednesday into Wednesday night will mark the wettest phase of\nthe 7-day period. Beginning Thursday, the main monsoonal\nmoisture axis will end, initiating a transition back to a warmer\nand drier pattern heading into the weekend and early next week.\nWhile lingering mid-level moisture will maintain low-end daily\nshower and thunderstorm chances over the higher terrain, overall\nconvective coverage will be isolated. Heat will quickly re-\nemerge as the primary operational impact, with high pressure\nbuilding back over the Intermountain West to push lower-\nelevation afternoon highs back into the mid-90s as early as\nFriday and continuing into early next week and likely much\nlonger.",
